CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the concept of vanishing points arising when a static camera observes an object translating at constant velocity. A bird flies away from camera center O along a straight receding path, depicted as a sequence of silhouettes with equal-length red velocity arrows labeled V (the bird's constant 3D velocity); it is the projected image-plane velocity arrows that grow shorter as the bird recedes. Green projection rays from O through the image plane to each successive bird position show that as the bird recedes, its image on the plane converges toward a single point labeled Vanishing point, and the image-plane velocity arrows shrink and all point toward that same location. The lower portion of the figure shows the corresponding image-plane view, where the bird projected positions collapse toward the vanishing point and the image velocity approaches zero. This figure demonstrates that for pure constant-velocity translation, the image positions and velocities converge toward the vanishing point of the motion direction — the focus of expansion — whose location encodes the direction of translation.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Camera center O: solid black sphere at the origin, lower-left of the scene
  - Text label 'O' just below the black sphere
  - Image plane: dark-gray outlined quadrilateral (vertical rectangle seen obliquely), standing a short distance in front of O along the optical axis, drawn as an unfilled parallelogram with only its four edges visible
  - 3D bird: white silhouette with black outline, travelling along a straight line that recedes from lower-left (near camera, larger on screen) to upper-right (far, smaller on screen)
  - Trail of 7–8 ghosted bird silhouettes marking equally-spaced-in-time positions along the 3D path, shrinking in apparent size as they recede
  - Thick red equal-length velocity arrows V drawn between consecutive 3D bird positions (constant 3D speed), all parallel to the path
  - Bold label 'V' above one of the 3D red arrows
  - Green projection rays: straight green lines from O through the image plane out to each 3D bird position (fanning upward/rightward, growing shallower as the bird recedes)
  - Projected bird images: small white/black bird silhouettes on the image plane where each green ray pierces it, crowding together toward the vanishing point
  - Thin short red image-velocity arrows between consecutive projected birds on the plane, each shorter than the last, all pointing toward the vanishing point
  - Vanishing point: small solid black dot on the image plane, with the thin red arrow chain pointing at it
  - Text label 'Vanishing point' below-right of that dot
  - Green direction ray from O through the vanishing point, extended far to the right, parallel to the bird's 3D velocity V
  - Thick red arrow labelled 'V' drawn on that extended green ray, illustrating the translation direction whose image is the vanishing point
  - Optional faint green dashed rays for bird positions that have already passed

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Right-handed world with camera center O at the origin (black sphere radius 0.09); optical axis along +Z (receding, screen-right-and-away), +X screen-right, +Y screen-up. Image plane: a flat rectangle at Z = f = 2.0, spanning X ∈ [-1.5, 1.5], Y ∈ [-1.2, 1.2], drawn as a dark-gray (#3A3A3A) wireframe outline only, no fill, exactly as in the source where only the four edges of the parallelogram are inked. Bird path: P(t) = P0 + V·t with P0 = (-0.7, 0.7, 1.4) and default V = (0.8, 0.15, 1.6) world units/s, t ∈ [0,5]; sample 8 trail positions at t = 0, 0.7, 1.4, ... 4.9. Bird silhouette ~0.35 units wide in world space, always facing along V, white fill with 3px black outline. Projection: p(t) = f · (Px/Pz, Py/Pz) placed on the plane at Z = f. Vanishing point = f · (Vx/Vz, Vy/Vz) = (1.0, 0.19, 2.0) for the default V — a solid black dot radius 0.035 with the text 'Vanishing point' set below and to the right in a serif face. Projected birds: same silhouette scaled by f/Pz, so they shrink from ~0.5 to ~0.07 of full size while marching from (-1.0, 1.0) toward (1.0, 0.19). Image-velocity arrows are the thin red (#E8262A, ~2px) segments between consecutive projected birds — lengths must be computed, not faked, and fall off like 1/Z². The 3D velocity arrows are thick red (#E8262A, ~9px shaft with a solid triangular head), all identical length |V|·Δt ≈ 1.26 units, and are drawn ABOVE/behind the bird trail running lower-left to upper-right; the bold 'V' label sits above the arrow near the middle of the trail. Green rays (#00D000, ~2px) run from O to each 3D bird position and visibly pass through the plane; rays for already-passed positions may be drawn at 35% opacity. A separate green ray leaves O through the vanishing point and continues to (5.0, 0.95, 10.0); a thick red arrow with a bold 'V' label sits on that ray in the lower-right of the frame, parallel to the 3D velocity arrows. Camera for the default view sits up, right and back so that O is at lower-left of frame, the plane reads as a leaning parallelogram, and the bird trail sweeps to the upper right across the top half of the canvas. Background white, all text black.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "That the projected birds lie on a real plane in 3D and each green line is a single ray through the camera center — the convergence is a depth effect, not a drawing trick."
  },
  {
    "id": "flightAnimation",
    "type": "animation",
    "teaches": "The bird moves at constant 3D speed, yet its image slows and creeps toward one fixed point — image velocity is not proportional to world velocity.",
    "label": "Fly at constant velocity V",
    "loop": true
  },
  {
    "id": "timeScrub",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Position along the straight 3D path maps to a specific image position and a specific image-velocity arrow length; equal time steps give shrinking image steps as depth Z grows.",
    "label": "Time t along flight path",
    "range": [
      0,
      5,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 1.2
  },
  {
    "id": "velocityDirection",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "The vanishing point is the image of the velocity direction alone: turn V and the focus of expansion jumps to f·(Vx/Vz, Vy/Vz), independent of where the bird started."
  },
  {
    "id": "viewMode",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "What the same motion looks like from outside (3D rays and plane) versus from the camera (a 2D image in which the bird shrinks toward the focus of expansion).",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "3D scene",
      "Camera image"
    ],
    "default": "3D scene"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Bird near the camera",
    "narration": "Early in the flight the bird is close and off to the left; its green ray pierces the image plane far from the vanishing point, and one time step moves the image a long way.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "timeScrub": 0.2
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Equal 3D steps, shrinking image steps",
    "narration": "Scrub forward: the red 3D arrows V stay the same length, but the red arrows between projected birds on the plane get shorter and shorter.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "timeScrub": 2
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Converging on the vanishing point",
    "narration": "As the bird recedes, its image crowds onto the black dot. The vanishing point is the limit of the projection as Z → ∞ and the image velocity tends to zero.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "timeScrub": 5
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "From the camera's seat",
    "narration": "Switch to the image plane itself: the whole flight is a track of shrinking birds sliding into the focus of expansion, with velocity arrows all aimed at it.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "Camera image",
      "timeScrub": 3
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Turn the velocity",
    "narration": "Drag V to a new direction and play the loop again — the vanishing point relocates to the image of the new direction, proving the FOE encodes the translation direction.",
    "state": {
      "viewMode": "3D scene",
      "timeScrub": 2.5
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 40,
  "elevationDeg": 18,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.7
}

NOTES: Use a THREE.PerspectiveCamera and rebuild ray/arrow geometry each frame from the parametric bird position rather than baking static meshes. Draw birds as flat textured planes or extruded 2D SVG-style shapes (THREE.Shape from the silhouette path) billboarded to face the viewer so the outline stays legible at all orbit angles; projected birds are the same Shape scaled by f/Pz and parented to the image-plane object so they stay coplanar. Green rays and red image-velocity arrows are Line2/LineSegments2 (fat lines) so they remain visible at thin widths; thick 3D V arrows are ArrowHelper-style cylinder+cone meshes in #E8262A. Labels 'O', 'V', 'Vanishing point' as CSS2DRenderer HTML labels with white halo so they never z-fight the wireframe. The velocity-direction drag should manipulate a handle on the extended green ray, clamping Vz > 0.3 so the vanishing point stays on the plane; recompute VP = f·(Vx/Vz, Vy/Vz) live and tween the dot. 'Camera image' toggle animates the camera to the O position looking down +Z (or crossfades to an orthographic overlay of the plane) and hides the 3D trail, leaving only projected birds, shrinking arrows and the FOE dot. Render the ghost trail with per-instance opacity; keep the plane's wireframe rendered with depthWrite off so rays crossing it read cleanly.

Follow the interaction plan above.

